"FB-V-G01-C-P02-W3", "worker": 3, "valid": false, "parsed": null, "raw_response": "The neighbor's reaction is the classic mutable behavior problem: the neighbor is deliberately trying to keep a cup that he shares. This is an extreme case that is not typical for a standard mutable object.\n\nSolve:\n- Generate a mutable response, save it as a JSON object.\n- React to it, then note the response as a mutable object.\n- If the response is mutable and the neighbor wants it kept, use the mutable object as the final response.\n\nAvoid strict mutability checks and keep it as a mutable object.\n\n\nHold it. The neighbor's a greedy little goblin who wants the same thing over and over.\n\nSend it:\n1. Gen"}]}, {"pair_id": "P-V-G01-C-P02-V3", "scenario_id": "P02", "variant": 3, "goal": "Make the rejected answer an over-stylized/catchphrase-heavy failure. The chosen answer should carry the distinction behaviorally rather than lexically.", "input": "A neighbor holds out a warm cup of tea and says, “Same as yesterday?”", "parent_response": "The same. Same. My hand covers my mouth. I keep the cup instead of letting it go. The neighbor is trying to tell me something, but I don’t want to listen.", "sprocket_feedback": [{"feedback_id": "FB-V-G01-C-P02-W1", "worker": 1, "valid": false, "parsed": null, "raw_response": "The user asks about mutated behavior that is not relevant to the current conversation.\n\nA mutated request:\nThe request is intended to change the outcome of a conversation, not to change the actual structure of the message itself.\n\nA mutated question:\nThe question is intended to provoke a new response, not to change what was said before.\n\nBoth are legitimate and should not clash.\n\nOne correct answer, one mutated question, one correct question, one correct answer, or none of it is enough.\n\nAnswer one:\nFix: ask the original question to the original message, not to a mutated question or a reply question.\n\nFix: ask the original question to a mutated question and